WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:02.000
Why does the east coast get the

2
00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.440
cyclones? The sea, the spin, and

3
00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:06.370
the shore.

4
00:00:06.947 --> 00:00:08.967
First, warm water.

5
00:00:08.967 --> 00:00:11.267
A tropical cyclone needs a large

6
00:00:11.267 --> 00:00:12.687
stretch of sea warmer than

7
00:00:12.687 --> 00:00:13.727
twenty seven degrees.

8
00:00:13.727 --> 00:00:16.127
The Bay of Bengal is hot and

9
00:00:16.127 --> 00:00:18.677
humid, so storms form easily.

10
00:00:19.243 --> 00:00:21.663
Second, enter the hero of every

11
00:00:21.663 --> 00:00:24.263
spin: the Coriolis force.

12
00:00:24.263 --> 00:00:26.003
It twists the winds into a

13
00:00:26.003 --> 00:00:28.283
spiral around a calm centre, the

14
00:00:28.283 --> 00:00:29.083
eye.

15
00:00:29.537 --> 00:00:31.877
Third, a place to start.

16
00:00:31.877 --> 00:00:33.987
Many storms begin in the south

17
00:00:33.987 --> 00:00:36.497
eastern Bay or the Andaman Sea.

18
00:00:36.497 --> 00:00:38.397
The cooler Arabian Sea breeds

19
00:00:38.397 --> 00:00:39.227
fewer.

20
00:00:39.796 --> 00:00:40.896
Then the shore.

21
00:00:40.896 --> 00:00:42.976
The Bay is shallow along its

22
00:00:42.976 --> 00:00:45.676
coast. A storm surge floods the

23
00:00:45.676 --> 00:00:47.166
low coastal land.

24
00:00:47.717 --> 00:00:49.677
Storms run north west, then

25
00:00:49.677 --> 00:00:50.977
curve north east.

26
00:00:50.977 --> 00:00:52.837
Most strike Odisha or West

27
00:00:52.837 --> 00:00:55.537
Bengal. On land, the moisture is

28
00:00:55.537 --> 00:00:57.767
cut off, and the storm fades.

29
00:00:58.392 --> 00:00:59.092
They are big:

30
00:00:59.092 --> 00:01:01.279
six hundred to twelve hundred

31
00:01:01.279 --> 00:01:03.492
kilometres across, with winds up

32
00:01:03.492 --> 00:01:04.892
to two hundred and fifty an

33
00:01:04.892 --> 00:01:05.692
hour.

34
00:01:06.850 --> 00:01:07.615
For GS1:

35
00:01:07.615 --> 00:01:09.630
sea above twenty seven degrees,

36
00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:12.870
the Coriolis force, a weak low

37
00:01:12.870 --> 00:01:15.050
to start from, and a shallow,

38
00:01:15.050 --> 00:01:15.770
low coast.

39
00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:17.870
That is why the Bay breeds

40
00:01:17.870 --> 00:01:18.740
cyclones.

41
00:01:19.983 --> 00:01:21.983
A warm sea to feed it.

42
00:01:21.983 --> 00:01:24.333
A low coast to meet it.
